course: dictionary
lesson: 13746
title: Interchangeable
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: When two things are interchangeable, you can swap one for the other and nothing important changes. Think of two matching batteries: either one fits the torch and it still works the same way. «Either pen will do; they are interchangeable.»
explain: Words can be interchangeable too. In «The room was big» and «The room was large», big and large mean nearly the same, so they are often interchangeable. To test it, put one word in place of the other and read the sentence aloud. If it still makes sense and means the same thing, the words are interchangeable in that sentence.
explain: Be careful, because some swaps change the meaning. In «She has five apples and two pears», you cannot swap apples and pears and keep the same meaning. In maths, 3 + 5 and 5 + 3 give the same answer, so the numbers are interchangeable in order. But 5 − 2 and 2 − 5 are not the same, so in subtraction they are not interchangeable.
fact: Interchangeable means able to be swapped for each other without making any difference.
fact: Things that are interchangeable can replace each other, like matching batteries, lids or Lego pieces.
fact: The phrase «interchangeable with» is useful: «Big is interchangeable with large in many sentences.»
fact: In addition and multiplication the numbers are interchangeable in order, but in subtraction and division they are not.
fact: A quick check is to swap the two things and ask: does it still work, and does it still mean the same?
exercise: Two batteries fit the same torch and work just as well. What word describes them? | Wobbly | Enormous | Curious | *Interchangeable
exercise: In 4 + 6 = 6 + 4, what does interchangeable show about the numbers? | The numbers are all odd | *The numbers can swap places in addition | The numbers can never change | The numbers are always taken away
exercise: Which word could be swapped for «large» in «a large dinner» without changing the meaning? | Blue | Tiny | *Big | Hungry
test: Can 5 − 2 and 2 − 5 be swapped without changing the answer? | Yes, they always give the same answer | Only if you write them in pencil | Yes, because both have a 5 | *No, subtraction gives a different answer when the numbers swap
test: Which phrase shows that two things are interchangeable? | *Interchangeable with | Far away from | Very, very tired | Once upon a time
exam: If two lids fit either jar and work just as well, what are the lids? | Broken | Neither lid is useful | Enormous | *Interchangeable
